%I A399625 #19 Oct 15 2026 18:53:50
%S A399625 0,0,0,0,0,0,1,6,22,78,257,503,824,1170,1665,2244,3010,3896,5028
%N A399625 Number of inequivalent ways (up to rotations and reflections of the board) to place 2 pawns on an n X n board such that n+2 nonattacking queens can be placed on the remaining squares.
%C A399625 The pawns are blockers only: queens do not attack pawns and pawns do not attack queens. A pawn interrupts a line of attack, so two queens on the same row, column or diagonal do not attack each other if a pawn lies between them (the convention of A129551). This is the k = 2 case of the n+k queens problem (Chatham et al.); cf. A103330 (k = 1) and A129553 (k = 3).
%C A399625 Pawn placements are counted up to the dihedral group of the square; A399501 counts them individually. This is to A399501 what A129552 is to A129551.
%H A399625 R. D. Chatham, <a href="https://www.npluskqueens.info/">The N+k Queens Problem Page</a>.
%H A399625 R. D. Chatham, M. Doyle, G. H. Fricke, J. Reitmann, R. D. Skaggs and M. Wolff, <a href="https://www.npluskqueens.info/uploads/2/1/3/5/21355572/queenssep2.pdf">Independence and Domination Separation on Chessboard Graphs</a>, J. Combin. Math. Combin. Comput. 68 (2009), 3-17.
%e A399625 For n = 7 there is a single pawn placement up to symmetry, {(2,2),(4,4)}, so a(7) = 1.
%Y A399625 Cf. A399501, A129551, A129552.
%K A399625 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A399625 1,8
%A A399625 _Jonathan Carroll_, Oct 03 2026
